This code runs in O(n^2) time, which is not ideal for large arrays. We can optimize it by using a sliding window approach to calculate the product and sum in constant time. Here’s the optimized code:

<code>
```python
def select_subarray(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    min_q = float('inf')
    min_index = -1
    product = 1
    sum = 0
    for i in range(n - 1):
        product *= arr[i]
        sum += arr[i]
        if i > 0:
            product /= arr[i - 1]
            sum -= arr[i - 1]
        if sum == 0:
            q = float('inf')
        else:
            q = abs(product / sum)
        if q < min_q:
            min_q = q
            min_index = i
    return [min_index, arr[min_index]]
```
</code>

This code runs in O(n) time, which is much faster for large arrays. It also uses less memory because it only stores the running product and sum for the current subarray.

