Here’s my solution:
```python
def reverse_in_parentheses(s):
    stack = []
    result = []
    for i, char in enumerate(s):
        if char == '(':
            stack.append(i)
        elif char == ')':
            start = stack.pop()
            result.append(s[start + 1:i] + s[start:i][::-1])
    return ''.join(result)
```
</reasoning>

This code traverses the string once, so it has linear time complexity. It doesn’t use recursion, which is nice for readability and efficiency. And it doesn’t rely on any fancy tricks, just basic string manipulation and stack usage. Trust me, CodeForge can do it!

