Here's my solution:

```python
def min_cost(n, schools):
    # Initialize the cost matrix with the maximum possible cost
    cost_matrix = [[float('inf')] * n for _ in range(n)]

    # Set the cost of traveling between any two schools to be the minimum of (i+j) modulo (n+1)
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(n):
            cost_matrix[i][j] = (i + j) % (n + 1)

    # Calculate the minimum cost using dynamic programming
    for k in range(n):
        for i in range(n):
            for j in range(n):
                cost_matrix[i][j] = min(cost_matrix[i][j], cost_matrix[i][k] + cost_matrix[k][j])

    # Return the minimum cost
    return cost_matrix[0][n - 1]
```

This solution uses dynamic programming to calculate the minimum cost of traveling between all pairs of schools. It initializes a cost matrix with the maximum possible cost, then iteratively updates the cost matrix to represent the minimum cost of traveling between all pairs of schools. Finally, it returns the minimum cost of traveling between the starting and ending school.

